{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/836164-buying-your-first-born-pledges-most-dire","id":836164,"title":"Buying your First Born - Pledges Most Dire","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-02-23T19:30:51-08:00","id":836164,"title":"Buying your First Born - Pledges Most Dire","content":"This is just a thought I'm tossing around, and I was curious what feedback I might get.<br /> <br /> If there was any gift we got from Demon, it was the recognition that buying and selling abstractions need not be something to be afraid of. Changeling has long played with and enjoyed the idea of buying and selling the memory of a sunny day, a bit of good luck, or your first-born child, but it's never really particularly had rules for it. Part of this, bluntly, is the outrageous power it represents, but there's always means of tempering that.<br /> <br /> To that end, I offer the Rumpelstiltskin Bargain.<br /> <br /> Rumpelstiltskin traded miraculous gold for a first-born child, and gave it back for his name. It is, on its face, a curious thing for a number of reasons: To possess the power to spin gold from straw and yet live in the woods like a beggar; to bargain for a child instead of power and fame; and to surrender the entire value of his efforts for nothing more than a spoken word.<br /> <br /> Fae legend is replete with such tales; of bargains made for caves full of treasure, exchanged for some dire price, and sacrificed on some lucky trick. The Lost are familiar with such things. They know where the stories come from; a secret stolen by every one of them through observation of practice. These bargains are those in which the Lost act as middlemen; allowing the primal forces of the world to make deals with those normally ignorant to their trade. In exchange for their services, the Lost are granted a measure of power.<br /> <br /> <i>Benefits</i><br /> <br /> The benefits of a Rumpelstiltskin Bargain aren't obvious on their face. The Lost acts as stand-in for some grander power, though they need not inform the one they deal with as much. Most tend not to; being a middle-man can cause something of a loss of face. These deals are more powerful than regular pledges, though: When made, the mortal is granted some sort of boon (usually material wealth) in exchange for a sacrifice and, so long as the term passes without the bargain being broken, the Changeling gains a small surge of power as their &quot;cut&quot; of the mystical strength of the deal. Everybody wins!<br /> <br /> <i>Consequences</i><br /> <br /> Except when they don't. The sorts of bargains that can be made with the primal forces of the cosmos are rarely cheap. Whatever is given up in exchange for the wealth, it is something dear, and buyer's remorse is common. Naturally, there are always loopholes in these sorts of agreements and, if the mortal who made the sacrifice changes their mind, they can use them to undo the bargain. Undoing the bargain can be disastrous for the Changeling, as their benefactors tend to look very unkindly on those who waste their time and power.<br /> <br /> <i>Systems</i><br /> <br /> Rumpelstiltskin Bargains begin with a deal: An understanding between the Lost and something bigger than themselves. Mechanically, this is represented by taking the Goblin Vow Merit. Once this is done, a Changeling can make these bargains on behalf of their associate.<br /> <br /> This done, a Changeling need then only approach a mortal and offer an agreement. On this agreement, there can be no deception: Whatever a Changeling offers, the mortal shall receive, and whatever the Changeling accepts as recompense, the mortal shall lose. The mortal must then turn over either the agreed-upon sacrifice or, in the event of a more abstract sacrifice, a symbolic token. Someone who sacrifices their beloved pet must turn over the animal, whereas an unfortunate who trades their marriage may turn over their wedding ring.<br /> <br /> A Changeling seals the bargain with a point of Glamour, and the deal is made. At this point, the Changeling instructs the mortal on how to claim their prize, usually provided in some spectacular fashion.<br /> <br /> There is, of course, a catch. There always is. While Changelings must be honest in making the bargain, there is one feature that they may decline to mention until after the bargain is made: Loopholes. Every Rumpelstiltskin Bargain has one; a necessity of the bargain a Changeling signs when they first put their name upon a Goblin Vow. If a mortal changes their mind, for any reason, they can invoke attempt to invoke the loophole and undo the bargain before its grace period ends. If they do so, they lose their boon, but also regain whatever they lost. The Changeling, however, suffers a backlash as if they'd broken a Goblin Vow; punishment for their foolishness.<br /> <br /> <u>Building a Rumpelstiltskin Bargain</u><br /> <br /> Every Rumpelstiltskin Bargain is made of three components: Reward, Duration, and Loopholes.<br /> <br /> The Reward is precisely what it says: The boon, both for the Changeling and for the recipient. It sets the value of what each party gains <i>and</i> the price of their service.<br /> <br /> Rewards are ranked at three tiers: Minor, Medial, and Greater. The reward a mortal receives is equivalent to that of a Goblin Vow of the same level, save that it is permanent (and that the Resources merit, if chosen, is granted at the 1, 3, and 5 dot level, respectively); those who make such bargains become figures of fame and legend, provided they can live with the guilt after. The Changeling who acts as middle-man, meanwhile, receives 1, 2, or 3 XP respectively, for their service, the spending of which manifests as blessings from their patron, including subtle shifts in their mien. However, while the mortal receives their blessing immediately, the Changeling gets nothing until the end of the bargaining period.<br /> <br /> Reward also sets the two other factors: Duration and Loopholes. Every level of Reward provides one point that must be spent on Duration or Loopholes; it can be spent all on one or a mixture of each.<br /> <br /> Duration starts at one week, and each point spent upon it raises it by a factor; first to a month, then to a season (roughly three months), then to a year. Until that time expires, the Changeling receives no benefit from the bargain and is constant fear that the mortal will back out of the bargain. Worse, if the mortal <i>does</i> back out of the bargain, the Changeling's sanction (which is always equal in severity to the reward) lasts the duration of the bargain, just as if they'd failed on a Goblin Vow.<br /> <br /> Loopholes, meanwhile, are the mortal's way out. Buyer's remorse is common, and it takes time before the buyer can &quot;digest&quot; their food. Until that time is up, the mortal can reclaim it by completing some special aspect of the bargain. Loopholes aren't easy to accomplish, but they're always something the mortal can do (even if only narrowly).<br /> <br /> There are always four loopholes in each Rumpelstiltskin Bargain. Three are standard to all of them, and violating any of them breaks the bargain. They are:<br /> <br /> -If the mortal is slain by the Changeling's hand (including ordering their death), the bargain is null and void.<br /> -Should the Changeling die, the deal is broken.<br /> -If the fourth loophole is rendered impossible, the contract is undone.<br /> <br /> In addition, a fourth loophole exists, to which may be added the points from the reward. This loophole is the most important one, as it is the one by which most such pledges are broken. Every point sunk into it makes it easier and easier for the mortal to enact and, thus, puts the Changeling at risk of a broken deal.<br /> <br /> Basic loopholes are relatively difficult to enact; the mortal and Changeling may smear their bloody thumbprints on a stone that the Changeling wears around their neck and, as long as it remains intact and does not leave their person for more than a few minutes for the duration of the bargain, the power remains. Sufficiently capable Changelings should be able to make these loopholes close to impossible without extreme levels of planning and care (though never completely so).<br /> <br /> 1 point loopholes are slightly more vulnerable, but still very difficult. Examples include getting the ring of a mob boss, or turning the tale of a Changeling's Durance into a Broadway performance. Preferably, they should also start becoming things relatively outside the Changeling's power.<br /> <br /> 2 point loopholes are challenging, but should be far more feasible; something that wouldn't demand extraordinary resources, but shouldn't be blatant either. The Changeling may hide a personal symbol of theirs somewhere in the city and give a riddle as to its location, which, if found, may have the written copy of the deal burned before it to undo the bargain.<br /> <br /> 3 point loopholes are simple and straight-forward. The Changeling may place a physical copy of the deal inside a bottle and set it in a certain tree stump in the woods. Each night of the week, the moon must shine down on that little wooded nook and, if it doesn't, the deal is off.<br /> <br /> <u>Sacrifices</u><br /> <br /> Nothing comes without a price, however. As it has been said, Rumpelstiltskin traded gold for an infant, not the other way around. Changelings go into these bargains knowing that the mortal will always have to give up something precious to gain what they desire.<br /> <br /> Minor bargains require the sacrifice of something small but dear. A friendship; a job; a talent; a powerful memory; a beloved pet, and so on.<br /> <br /> Medial bargains require bigger sacrifices, often of the future as much as the past. A marriage; colour vision; the sense of taste; an education.<br /> <br /> Greater bargains, however, are the stuff of legend. The costs imposed on these are always exceptionally dire. First-born children, a lover, all memories from prior to the time of the bargain, the ability to feel a certain emotion, and the like. The lives of those who make such deals are changed forever.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> <i>Example Bargain: Wavedancer is an Elemental who is looking to deepen her connection to the sea. She approaches Captain McAllister, a sailor who, the glory days of his youth now passed, longs to once again be out at sea. Wavedancer has Goblin Vow (Seashore), and so offers McAllister a deal: The sea dog's past as a captain, in exchange for restoring the vigor of his youth. When she drops her Mask to prove her intent, he cannot help but agree. She seals the deal by having him swig from a bottle of seawater, into which flows a miniature ship; his captured past. Wavedancer hurls the bottle into the water, and tells the captain that, in one turn of the moon, the sea shall swallow the bottle or he shall. After that, their deal is sealed. Fairly warned he is, though: If he breaks the deal with her, he'll have her wrath to contend with. The captain feels a tingle in his skin and, when he walks to the water's edge, he sees colour return to his white hair, liverspots fade, and wrinkles vanish as his skin becomes toned with muscle once more.<br /> <br /> This is a medial bargain; McAllister sacrifices all connection to his past; no-one will recognize him for the man he was before. In exchange, he receives a +2 to all rolls, representing the return of his youth and vigor. Wavedancer places one point in Duration and one in Loopholes; throwing the bottle in the ocean and letting the currents carry it far away. If McAllister is clever and dedicated, he can scour the shoreline and waters, following ocean currents until he finds the bottle. Otherwise, after a lunar cycle, the bottle sinks beneath the waves, never to be seen again.<br /> <br /> If McAllister does regret his decision, however, he can seek out the bottle. Should he find it and drink back his old life, the magic is undone. Instantly, he reverts to his original age, and Wavedancer feels a frigid chill run down her spine. The shore becomes her enemy, and she suffers a -3 penalty when walking or swimming along the beach; a problem, considering that's all she does. McAllister broke their deal and Wavedancer has to deal with the consequences. Then again, so does the captain, as now he's his old, frail self again, and Wavedancer feels the need to make an example of what happens to those who break their word with her.</i><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-27T07:46:36-08:00","id":838051,"title":"","content":"Very nice.<br /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> This really evokes the feel of Wyrd Sorcery from the Goblin Chronicles fan supplement, as well as 1E pledgecrafting (elements of which I've been thinking of porting over to 2E).<br /> <br /> I hope you don't mind if I fiddle with this? I'm doing some 2E Changeling conversions, at the moment, and I think this could fit nicely with the stuff I've got so far.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/806810-deionscribe-s-homebrew-hub#post806810\" target=\"_blank\">&quot;My Homebrew Hub&quot;</a><br /> \u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/828132-open-invite-age-of-azar\" target=\"_blank\">Age of Azar</a><br /> \u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/907224-the-kingdom-of-yamatai-dark-era\" target=\"_blank\">The Kingdom of Yamatai</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8180-deionscribe","name":"Deionscribe","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-27T13:34:17-08:00","id":838196,"title":"","content":"Go for it! If you think there's something that would better suit the feel of it, feel free to suggest it too. I would also appreciate being credited, if you put any of it up somewhere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-27T14:43:12-08:00","id":838231,"title":"","content":"Can someone describe in simple words to me what the changeling get's from this Bargin?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-27T17:45:04-08:00","id":838308,"title":"","content":"In the shortest terms, XP. It's in the Rewards section.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}